			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4468835">
									

<p>My biggest gripe with this article is that the description spoils pretty much everything about the &quot;mystery&quot;: exploration logs aren't really interesting when I already know <em>what</em> is happening and <em>who</em> is responsible for it. The only question left is <em>why</em>, which is also answered pretty quickly. On the other hand, the parts of the scip that I found interesting (lorem ipsum and other gibberish replacing parts of speech; Joe convincing <em>the entire town</em> to give gifts to his son) are rather short.</p>
<p>Also, I don't see a reason for including chroniton particles. I initially thought they were going to be important to the story, but they are only mentioned once after the introduction to explain the agents' death (which doesn't seem to serve any purpose). I don't think anything would be lost if the chronitons were scrapped altogether and the tapes were replaced with the agents' field notes. It's not like the damage to tapes is important: the various &quot;The rest of the tape is unsalvageable&quot; don't really add any suspence and the exploration log already reads like a whole coherent story anyway.</p>

							</div>

								
					
